  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers likely today. Isolated thunderstorms \nalso possible across mostly northern areas this afternoon. \nLittle drought relief expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAt the surface, a cold front will cross the region today. \nAloft, a disturbance will cross the region today in advance of \nan approaching trof. Showers will occur across the region today\nwith these features. Lapse rates steepen with the upper level \ndisturbance this afternoon, with CAPE values of 300 to 500 J/kg \npossible. Enough instability should exist this afternoon to \nalso support the possibility of isolated thunderstorms across \nmostly northern areas. Cold air aloft could also possibly \nsupport small hail with any thunderstorms. Additional rain \ntotals today will generally be less than a quarter inch, \nproviding little drought relief. Showers end this evening. The \nupper trof crosses the region Friday while a disturbance moves \nthrough the trof supporting another chance of showers.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm temperatures and gusty winds this weekend \nbefore the next rain chances early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure builds over the region Saturday and moves off the\ncoast to our south Sunday. Gusty winds out of the southwest will\ndevelop between the high and the weakened remnants of Isaias as\nit moves into the Ohio valley Saturday into Sunday. These winds\nwill help to funnel warmer temperatures (upper 60s to low \n70s) into the area for Sunday and Monday, potentially 10-15 \ndegrees above average for this time of year. As the system moves\noff the southern New England coast, it could bring a chance of \nshowers for the Downeast coast Sunday night into Monday. Rain \nchances could decrease if the system stays further south. A \nseparate low pressure develops over the Great Lakes Monday night\nas a trough deepens behind the departing tropical remnants. \nThis will bring a chance of rain for the beginning of next week.",
